ms.\\\"\\n\\nDo not attempt to prove or refute it yet. State the claim exactly as given and note any ambiguities you see in its wording.\", \"additional_kwargs\": {}, \"response_metadata\": {}, \"type\": \"human\", \"name\": null, \"id\": \"bbcc59f7-f6bd-4db2-bed4-15dfb571432b\", \"example\": false}]]", "gen_ai.output.messages": "{\"generations\": [[{\"text\": \"## Claim (recorded verbatim)\\n\\n> \\\"For any two numeric columns A and B in a spreadsheet, the sum of their element-wise differences equals the difference of their column sums.\\\"\\n\\nNo proof or refutation is attempted here.\\n\\n## Ambiguities in the wording\\n\\n1. **Row alignment / equal length.** \\\"Element-wise\\\" presupposes that A and B are paired row-by-row and have the same number of entries. The claim does not state whether A and B must be aligned on a shared row index, or what happens if the columns have different lengths (e.g., trailing blanks).\\n\\n2. **Sign / ordering of the differences.** \\\"Element-wise differences\\\" could mean `A_i \\u2212 B_i` or `B_i \\u2212 A_i`. Likewise \\\"difference of their column sums\\\" could mean `sum(A) \\u2212 sum(B)` or `sum(B) \\u2212 sum(A)`. The claim does not fix an order, which matters for stating it precisely (the intended reading is presumably consistent: same order on both sides).\\n\\n3. **NaNs, blanks, and non-numeric entries.** \\\"Numeric columns\\\" says nothing about missing values. It is unclear whether rows with a blank/NaN in either column are dropped pairwise, treated as zero, or propagated.\\n\\n4. **Scope of \\\"any two \\u2026 in a spreadsheet.\\\"** Ambiguous between:\\n   - *within a single given spreadsheet*, any pair of numeric columns; and\\n   - *across all spreadsheets*, any pair of numeric columns.\\n   The formalization should quantify explicitly rather than leave \\\"a spreadsheet\\\" vague.\\n\\n5. **Header/text rows.** \\\"In a spreadsheet\\\" does not say whether header rows or other non-data rows are part of the columns. The claim presumably refers to the data entries only.\\n\\n6. **\\\"Equals.\\\"** Unspecified whether this means exact arithmetic equality over reals/integers, or equality within floating-point tolerance. This matters if columns contain floats.\\n\\n7. **Column identity.** It is unstated whether A and B must be distinct columns, and whether the columns are identified by name or position.\\n\\n## Minimal formalization shape (for later use)\\n\\nGiven a finite index set `I` of aligned rows, with `A = (a_i)_{i\\u2208I}` and `B = (b_i)_{i\\u2208I}`, the claim is: `\\u03a3_{i\\u2208I} (a_i \\u2212 b_i) = (\\u03a3_{i\\u2208I} a_i) \\u2212 (\\u03a3_{i\\u2208I} b_i)`. The above ambiguities must be resolved to fix `I`, the operand order, and the treatment of missing/non-numeric entries before this becomes a precise statement.\\n\\nLet me know if you'd like me to confirm the verbatim text against a source, or to fix these ambiguities into a single canonical formal statement.\", \"generation_info\": {\"finish_reason\": \"stop\", \"logprobs\": null}, \"type\": \"ChatGeneration\", \"message\": {\"content\": \"## Claim (recorded verbatim)\\n\\n> \\\"For any two numeric columns A and B in a spreadsheet, the sum of their element-wise differences equals the difference of their column sums.\\\"\\n\\nNo proof or refutation is attempted here.\\n\\n## Ambiguities in the wording\\n\\n1. **Row alignment / equal length.** \\\"Element-wise\\\" presupposes that A and B are paired row-by-row and have the same number of entries. The claim does not state whether A and B must be aligned on a shared row index, or what happens if the columns have different lengths (e.g., trailing blanks).\\n\\n2. **Sign / ordering of the differences.** \\\"Element-wise differences\\\" could mean `A_i \\u2212 B_i` or `B_i \\u2212 A_i`. Likewise \\\"difference of their column sums\\\" could mean `sum(A) \\u2212 sum(B)` or `sum(B) \\u2212 sum(A)`. The claim does not fix an order, which matters for stating it precisely (the intended reading is presumably consistent: same order on both sides).\\n\\n3. **NaNs, blanks, and non-numeric entries.** \\\"Numeric columns\\\" says nothing about missing values. It is unclear whether rows with a blank/NaN in either column are dropped pairwise, treated as zero, or propagated.\\n\\n4. **Scope of \\\"any two \\u2026 in a spreadsheet.\\\"** Ambiguous between:\\n   - *within a single given spreadsheet*, any pair of numeric columns; and\\n   - *across all spreadsheets*, any pair of numeric columns.\\n   The formalization should quantify explicitly rather than leave \\\"a spreadsheet\\\" vague.\\n\\n5. **Header/text rows.** \\\"In a spreadsheet\\\" does not say whether header rows or other non-data rows are part of the columns. The claim presumably refers to the data entries only.\\n\\n6. **\\\"Equals.\\\"** Unspecified whether this means exact arithmetic equality over reals/integers, or equality within floating-point tolerance. This matters if columns contain floats.\\n\\n7. **Column identity.** It is unstated whether A and B must be distinct columns, and whether the columns are identified by name or position.\\n\\n## Minimal formalization shape (for later use)\\n\\nGiven a finite index set `I` of aligned rows, with `A = (a_i)_{i\\u2208I}` and `B = (b_i)_{i\\u2208I}`, the claim is: `\\u03a3_{i\\u2208I} (a_i \\u2212 b_i) = (\\u03a3_{i\\u2208I} a_i) \\u2212 (\\u03a3_{i\\u2208I} b_i)`. The above ambiguities must be resolved to fix `I`, the operand order, and the treatment of missing/non-numeric entries before this becomes a precise statement.\\n\\nLet me know if you'd like me to confirm the verbatim text against a source, or to fix these ambiguities into a single canonical formal statement.\", \"additional_kwargs\": {\"refusal\": null}, \"response_metadata\": {\"token_usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 1013, \"prompt_tokens\": 322, \"total_tokens\": 1335, \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"accepted_prediction_tokens\": null, \"audio_tokens\": n